Finding: {"title":"Void/refund calls to MiCamp are not idempotent and DB update happens after the external call with no compensation on partial failure","severity":"HIGH","area":"Idempotency / distributed transaction correctness","files":["src/server/routes/routes-micamp.ts","src/lib/micamp.ts"],"detail":"In both void (L625-652) and refund (L697-730), the sequence is: call MiCamp's live API, then update the DB. If the process crashes or the response times out between the successful MiCamp call and the DB write (or if the DB write itself fails), MiCamp has voided/refunded money but the local `paymentTransactions` row still shows `status: 'completed'` — nothing indicates the discrepancy exists, and a retry by the client would call void/refund AGAIN since the guard at L610/L679 checks the stale local status. There is no idempotency key sent to MiCamp itself (`postTransaction` in micamp.ts never includes a client-generated idempotency/reference key), so MiCamp has no way to dedupe a retried void/refund request either.","impact":"Double-void or double-refund on retry after a transient failure; permanent state drift between MiCamp's ledger and the local `paymentTransactions` table with no reconciliation mechanism.","recommendation":"Wrap the DB status update and MiCamp call in a way that tolerates partial failure — at minimum, write a 'pending' void/refund record BEFORE calling MiCamp, then finalize on success/failure, and pass an idempotency key derived from the local transaction id so a retried MiCamp call is deduped server-side.","confidence":"medium"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

